				<attribute key="anchor" type="String">c</attribute>
			</section>
		</section>
		<section name="node">
			<attribute key="id" type="int">59</attribute>
			<attribute key="label" type="String">Following is what I come up with

We have 2 major phases in the implementation.
Phase 1: One is to read the elements of the the array.
Phase 2: Second is finding the sub array

Phase 1
1. Read the length of the array(let's call it n).
2. Read the elements of the array.
Phase 2
6. Create a variable maxcount and initialize it to 1 (Why not 0?).
7. Create a variable called currCount and initialize it to 1.
8. Create a variable called startIndex and endIndex and initialize both of them to 0.
9. Create a variable called currStartIndex and initilize it to i
10. Create a variable i and initialize it to 1
11. Now compare the value at index i to the value at index i-1.
11.1. If value at index i is greater then increase the value of currCount by 1 
11.2 Otherwise, 
11.2.1 Compare the value of currCount with maxCount
11.2.2 If maxCount is smaller than currCount then update the value of maxCount with currCount, update value of endIndex with i-1 (why not i?) and update value of startIndex with currStartIndex.
11.2.3 Otherwise do nothing
11.2.4 Initialize currCount with 1 again and and currStartIndex with 1 again.
12. Move to next element of array by increasing value of i by 1
13 Repeat steps 11,11.1,11.2,11.2.1,11.2.2,11.2.3,11.2.4,12 till we reach the end of array.
14. After coming out of the loop, compare the value of currCount with maxCount
14.1. If maxCount is smaller than currCount then update the value of maxCount with currCount, update value of endIndex with n-1 (why not n?) and update value of startIndex with currStartIndex.
15. Display maxCount, startIndex and endIndex.</attribute>
